---
name: wsinsight-train
description: Operate the wsitrain CLI to train WSInsight cell-classification heads from paired Xenium + H&E cohorts and export a deployable model folder
---

# wsinsight-train — Agentic AI Skill File

> **Purpose**: Enable an agentic AI (Claude, OpenClaw, Hermes, or any
> tool-using LLM agent) to autonomously operate `wsitrain` — turning a cohort of
> paired Xenium and H&E samples into a `models/<tissue>/main/` folder that
> WSInsight loads with `--zoo-model-dir`.

---

## 1. What Is wsinsight-train?

`wsitrain` is a headless training CLI. It labels cells from Xenium, segments
nuclei on the matching H&E, transfers the labels across the registration,
cuts training data, trains a classifier head, and exports a deployable model
folder.

- **Command**: `wsitrain`
- **Python**: ≥ 3.11
- **Entry points**: `wsitrain` (CLI), `wsinsight-train-mcp` (MCP server)
- **Product**: `models/<tissue>/main/` — consumed by
  `wsinsight run --zoo-model-dir <that path>`

### 1.1 Registration is consumed, not produced

`wsitrain` does **not** register Xenium to H&E. It reads a registration that
already exists (`outs/registration_params.json`, optionally
`outs/direct_transf.txt`), produced by `ST2WSI_Registration`. If those files
are missing, the `transfer` stage has nothing to work with. Never offer to
"run the registration" from this CLI.

### 1.2 Two model shapes, one pipeline

`--object-detection` is the switch, and it decides which cutting stage runs and
which trainer is used:

| | `end2end` (default) | `stardist` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cutting stage | `tile` — 1024 px tiles | `crop` — one centred crop per cell |
| Trainer | CellViT, via `$CELLVIT_ROOT` | any `torchvision` classifier |
| Exported config | `object_detection: {name: end2end}` | `{name: stardist, ...}` |
| Needs `$CELLVIT_ROOT` | yes | no |

The stage that does not apply reports itself as skipped; that is expected, not
an error.

---

## 2. Assumed Install

Assume `wsitrain` is already installed, and verify with `wsitrain --version`
before anything else. Installing is the fallback, not the normal path.

```bash
bash ./conda-setup.sh wsitrain          # torch + cellpose + kurtorank + wsitrain
```

Two environment prerequisites bite in practice, and both fail *late* — after
minutes of work — so check them before starting a run:

| Requirement | Needed by | Symptom if missing | Escape hatch |
| ----------- | --------- | ------------------ | ------------ |
| `$CELLVIT_ROOT` pointing at a CellViT-plus-plus checkout | `train`, `export` on the `end2end` path | those stages raise | none — the crop-classifier path (`--object-detection stardist`) does not use it |
| A PTX toolchain (`ptxas` + `libdevice`) for TensorFlow | StarDist inside `segment` | `No PTX compilation provider is available` | `--stardist-cpu`, or install `nvidia-cuda-nvcc-cu12` |

```bash
export CELLVIT_ROOT=/path/to/CellViT-plus-plus
```

`wsitrain check` exercises both and is the cheapest way to find out. Run it
first.

---

## 3. Input Data Contract

A **sample** is any directory containing `outs/cells.parquet` with a sibling
H&E file matching `*_he_*image.ome.tif`. `wsitrain check` reports exactly what
it found and what it rejected.

Per sample:

| Path | Produced by | Used by |
| ---- | ----------- | ------- |
| `outs/cells.parquet` | Xenium | discovery, `transfer` |
| `outs/analysis/clustering/gene_expression_graphclust/clusters.csv` | Xenium | `annotate` |
| `outs/celltype_assignment_<task>[_label].csv` | `kurtorank annotate` | `transfer` |
| `outs/registration_params.json` (+ optional `outs/direct_transf.txt`) | `ST2WSI_Registration` | `transfer` |
| sibling `*_he_*image.ome.tif` | scanner | `segment`, `tile` / `crop` |

The `_label` suffix on the assignment CSV is **inconsistent by design**
(`pantissue`, `hne`, `pannuke`, `sthelar_*`, `lcp` carry it; `subtype`,
`major`, `hne_type` do not). `wsitrain` resolves both forms itself — do not
rename files to make them uniform.

---

## 4. CLI Reference

```bash
wsitrain --help
wsitrain <command> --help
```

| Command | Purpose |
| ------- | ------- |
| `check` | Preflight: validate input + environment. **Start here.** |
| `run` | Run the end-to-end pipeline. |
| `annotate` | Label cells with kurtorank. |
| `segment` | Segment nuclei on each H&E. |
| `transfer` | Join cell labels onto the H&E nuclei. |
| `tile` | Cut labelled slides into training tiles (`end2end`). |
| `crop` | Cut one centred crop per cell (`stardist`). |
| `split` | Build train/val lists and the CellViT config. |
| `train` | Train the classifier head. |
| `validate` | Score the trained head; write the confusion matrix. |
| `export` | Promote the best checkpoint to a deployable model. |
| `report` | Summarise the run. |
| `schema` | Emit a machine-readable JSON schema of every subcommand. |

### 4.1 The pipeline

```
annotate → segment → transfer → (tile | crop) → split → train → validate → export → report
```

Every stage is also a command taking the same `--input` / `--tissue` /
`--output`. A stage refuses to start until its prerequisites are done, so you
can step through one command at a time, or run them all with `run`.

`tile` and `crop` are alternatives, chosen by `--object-detection`.
`run --run-skip a b` drops named stages.

Completed stages are skipped on re-run (manifest-based). `--force` re-runs
stages the manifest already marks done.

### 4.2 Options common to every stage

| Option | Type | Default | Description |
| ------ | ---- | ------- | ----------- |
| `--input` | path | *required* | Cohort root containing the samples. |
| `--tissue` | string | `pantissue` | Tissue name; also namespaces the saved config and the model folder. |
| `--output` | path | derived | Output root. |
| `--force` | flag | off | Re-run stages the manifest already marks done. |
| `--config FILE` | path | none | YAML of settings, applied over the saved config and under these flags. |
| `--reset-config` | flag | off | Ignore the config an earlier command saved in `--output`; start from shipped defaults plus these flags. |
| `--show-config` | flag | off | List every setting and where its value came from, including defaults. |

### 4.3 How settings resolve — read this before passing flags

A stage command **only offers the flags its own stage reads**. Everything else
is inherited from `<output>/run-<tissue>.yaml`, which the previous command
wrote. Settings resolve through four layers, lowest priority first:

1. shipped defaults
2. the saved `run-<tissue>.yaml` record
3. a `--config FILE`
4. the flags you type

Each command prints where every setting came from; `--show-config` adds the
defaults.

`--config` **patches** the saved record rather than replacing it, so it is safe
for changing a couple of settings mid-pipeline. `--reset-config` drops the
saved layer: alone it means "start from the shipped defaults"; together with
`--config` it reproduces a run exactly from a saved `run-<tissue>.yaml`.

Practical consequence: if a flag you expect is "missing" from a stage command,
that stage does not read it — set it on the earlier stage that does, or pass it
through `--config`.

### 4.4 Four options with no defaults — on purpose

`--architecture`, `--patch-size-pixels`, `--patch-spacing-um-px` and
`--stain-normalization` define the **deployed model's geometry**. They have no
defaults because a wrong guess trains a model that scores well in `validate`
and then misclassifies under WSInsight. The config refuses to build without
them.

**Do not guess these.** Ask the user for the target geometry.

### 4.5 `wsitrain check`

```bash
wsitrain check --input /path/to/cohort --tissue pantissue
```

| Option | Type | Default | Description |
| ------ | ---- | ------- | ----------- |
| `--input` | path | *required* | Cohort root. |
| `--tissue` | string | `pantissue` | Tissue name. |
| `--output` | path | derived | Output root. |

Reports the samples it discovered, what each is missing, and whether the
environment (`$CELLVIT_ROOT`, GPU/PTX) supports the requested path. It is the
only command that is fast and side-effect-free.

### 4.6 `wsitrain run`

```bash
wsitrain run --input /path/to/cohort --tissue breast \
  --object-detection end2end --backbone SAM-H-x40 --gpus 0
```

`run` accepts the union of every stage's flags, plus:

| Option | Type | Default | Description |
| ------ | ---- | ------- | ----------- |
| `--run-skip STAGE [STAGE ...]` | list | none | Run everything **except** these stages. |

#### Stage flags, grouped by the stage that reads them

**`annotate`** — `--task` (label space, e.g. `pantissue` \| `hne` \|
`pannuke`), `--markers-csv` (default: kurtorank's bundled panel),
`--top-k-markers` (keep the K most specific genes per subtype).

**`segment`** — `--segmenter {cellpose,stardist}`, `--cellpose-model`,
`--stardist-model`, `--stardist-model-dir` (parent of the model folder; avoids
downloading — env fallback `WSITRAIN_STARDIST_DIR`, then `KERAS_HOME`),
`--stardist-cpu` / `--no-stardist-cpu`, `--diameter` (nucleus diameter in
**microns**; omit for auto), `--cellpose-batch-size` (lower to 4/2/1 if the GPU
OOMs), `--cellpose-flow-threshold` (`0` skips the GPU flow check, avoiding WSI
OOM), `--mpp`, `--transform`, `--gpus`,
`--stardist-normalization-pmin` / `--stardist-normalization-pmax`.

**`transfer`** — `--match-radius-px` (nucleus lookup radius in H&E px; `0` =
exact pixel), `--min-match-rate` (drop slides matching below this fraction),
`--drop-labels LABEL [LABEL ...]` (space- or comma-separated),
`--transform`, `--task`, `--mpp`.

**`tile`** (end2end) — `--min-cells` (drop tiles with fewer cells),
`--bg-thresh` (drop tiles whose mean RGB exceeds this), `--overlap` (fraction
0–1), `--tile-px`.

**`crop`** (stardist) — `--object-detection`, `--architecture`,
`--patch-size-pixels`, `--patch-spacing-um-px`,
`--stain-normalization` / `--no-stain-normalization`, `--norm-sample-size`
(cells sampled per slide to estimate the stain matrix),
`--stardist-normalization-pmin` / `--stardist-normalization-pmax`, `--mpp`.

**`split`** — `--val-frac`, `--by-tile` (default; hold out a fraction of tiles
from every slide) / `--by-slide` (hold out whole slides), `--seed`,
`--weight-cap` (cap on inverse-frequency class weights), `--backbone`,
`--fold`, `--task`, `--gpus`.

**`train`** — `--tune` (auto-tune iterations, `0` = off), `--backbone` (e.g.
`SAM-H-x40`), `--fold` (e.g. `fold_0`), `--gpus`, `--epochs`, `--batch-size`,
`--lr`, `--weight-decay`, `--pretrained` (start from torchvision ImageNet
weights), `--num-workers`.

**`validate`** — `--backbone`, `--fold`.

**`export`** — `--backbone`, `--fold`, `--tile-px`.

**`report`** — no stage-specific flags.

### 4.7 `--transform` decides which samples every stage sees

`--transform` takes `affine+bspline`, `affine`, or `none`, chosen by the
alignment state of the cohort. It is **not** a cosmetic setting: it filters
which samples are usable, so `segment` and `transfer` must be given the same
value. Setting it on one and not the other silently trains on a different
cohort than you validated.

### 4.8 `--gpus cpu` only disables the segmenter

`--gpus cpu` turns off GPU **segmentation**. CellViT training still needs a
real device index, and `split` rejects `cpu`. There is no fully-CPU end2end
training path.

### 4.9 Segmenter choice

`stardist` is the default segmenter, but its TensorFlow install is optional.
When it is skipped, `--segmenter cellpose` is the fallback. Do not treat a
missing StarDist as a broken install; check which segmenter the environment
actually has before switching.

### 4.10 Stain normalization

`crop` Macenko-normalises through the **same HistomicsTK calls WSInsight runs
at inference**, estimating one matrix per slide from `--norm-sample-size`
(default 256) cells. Estimation cost is linear in pixels, which is why it
samples; applying it costs about 1 ms/cell.

Each `cells/<tissue>/<sample>.h5` keeps **both** `images` (normalised — what
the model trains on) and `images_raw`, plus the matrices as attributes.

`--stardist-normalization-pmin/pmax` feed **both** `segment`'s `normalize()`
call and the exported config, so detection cannot drift between training and
inference. They default to csbdeep's 3.0 / 99.8.

### 4.11 `wsitrain schema`

```bash
wsitrain schema
```

Emits `{"schema_version": 1, "commands": {...}}` reflected from the same parser
the CLI runs, so it cannot drift. Read it rather than guessing a flag name.

---

## 5. Output Layout

Under `--output`:

```
run-<tissue>.yaml        # the saved settings record every later command inherits
manifest*                # which stages are done (drives skip-on-re-run)
logs/                    # per-stage logs
nuclei/                  # segmentation results
masks/                   # segmentation masks
cells/<tissue>/<sample>.h5   # per-cell crops: images (normalised) + images_raw
trainingset/             # tiles / crops, train+val lists, CellViT config
models/<tissue>/main/    # THE PRODUCT — pass to wsinsight --zoo-model-dir
report/                  # run summary + confusion matrix
```

Deploy with:

```bash
wsinsight run --wsi-dir slides/ --results-dir results/ \
  --zoo-model-dir <output>/models/<tissue>/main
```

---

## 6. Common Workflows

### 6.1 One-shot end-to-end run

```bash
export CELLVIT_ROOT=/path/to/CellViT-plus-plus
wsitrain check --input /data/cohort --tissue breast
wsitrain run   --input /data/cohort --tissue breast \
  --object-detection end2end --backbone SAM-H-x40 --gpus 0 \
  --transform affine+bspline
```

### 6.2 Step through, inspecting between stages

```bash
wsitrain annotate --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --task pantissue
wsitrain segment  --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --segmenter stardist --stardist-cpu
wsitrain transfer --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --transform affine+bspline --min-match-rate 0.5
wsitrain tile     --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --tile-px 1024 --min-cells 5
wsitrain split    --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --by-slide --val-frac 0.2
wsitrain train    --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --epochs 50 --gpus 0
wsitrain validate --input /data/cohort --tissue breast
wsitrain export   --input /data/cohort --tissue breast
wsitrain report   --input /data/cohort --tissue breast
```

Each command inherits the earlier settings from `run-breast.yaml`; you only
type what changes.

### 6.3 Crop-classifier model (no CellViT)

```bash
wsitrain run --input /data/cohort --tissue breast \
  --object-detection stardist \
  --architecture resnet50 \
  --patch-size-pixels 64 --patch-spacing-um-px 0.25 \
  --stain-normalization
```

### 6.4 Reproduce a past run exactly

```bash
wsitrain run --input /data/cohort --tissue breast \
  --reset-config --config /archive/run-breast.yaml
```

### 6.5 Change one setting mid-pipeline

```bash
wsitrain train --input /data/cohort --tissue breast --config tweak.yaml --force
```

---

## 7. Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
| ------- | ----- | --- |
| `train` / `export` raise about CellViT | `$CELLVIT_ROOT` unset | `export CELLVIT_ROOT=/path/to/CellViT-plus-plus`, or switch to `--object-detection stardist` |
| `No PTX compilation provider is available` | StarDist/TensorFlow has no PTX toolchain | `--stardist-cpu`, or install `nvidia-cuda-nvcc-cu12` |
| Config refuses to build | One of the four no-default options is unset | Supply `--architecture`, `--patch-size-pixels`, `--patch-spacing-um-px`, `--stain-normalization` |
| Nothing happens; stages report done | Manifest marks them complete | `--force` |
| A flag "does not exist" on a stage | That stage does not read it | Set it on the stage that does, or pass `--config` |
| Trained on a different cohort than validated | `--transform` differed between `segment` and `transfer` | Use the same value for both |
| `split` rejects `--gpus cpu` | CellViT training needs a device index | `--gpus cpu` only disables GPU segmentation |
| Cellpose OOM on a WSI | Batch too large / GPU flow check | Lower `--cellpose-batch-size` to 4/2/1; `--cellpose-flow-threshold 0` |
| StarDist downloads a model unexpectedly | No local model directory | `--stardist-model-dir`, or set `WSITRAIN_STARDIST_DIR` / `KERAS_HOME` |
| Most slides dropped at `transfer` | Registration matched poorly | Inspect the match rate; lower `--min-match-rate` only if the alignment is genuinely acceptable |
| Assignment CSV "not found" | The `_label` suffix is inconsistent | wsitrain tries both forms; check the file exists under either name rather than renaming |
| Where did this setting come from? | Four-layer resolution | `--show-config` |

---

## 8. Agent Decision Guide

```text
Is wsitrain installed?  (wsitrain --version)
├─ Yes → continue
└─ No  → fallback install (§2), then continue

ALWAYS: wsitrain check --input <cohort> --tissue <t>
├─ samples missing registration_params.json
│      → ST2WSI_Registration must run first; wsitrain cannot produce it
├─ samples missing celltype_assignment_*.csv
│      → wsitrain annotate (or kurtorank annotate) first
└─ environment gaps
       ├─ no $CELLVIT_ROOT → set it, or use --object-detection stardist
       └─ no PTX toolchain → --stardist-cpu, or --segmenter cellpose

Which model shape?
├─ CellViT head for wsinsight object-based inference → --object-detection end2end
│      needs $CELLVIT_ROOT; cutting stage is `tile`
└─ crop classifier over StarDist detections → --object-detection stardist
       needs --architecture / --patch-size-pixels / --patch-spacing-um-px /
       --stain-normalization, all supplied by the user; cutting stage is `crop`

One shot or stepwise?
├─ Cohort already validated by check, settings known → wsitrain run
└─ Anything uncertain → step through stage by stage and inspect
```

### Key constraints for agents

1. **Run `wsitrain check` first, every time.** It is cheap and catches the
   failures that otherwise appear an hour in.
2. **Never guess `--architecture`, `--patch-size-pixels`,
   `--patch-spacing-um-px`, or `--stain-normalization`.** They have no defaults
   deliberately; a wrong value produces a model that validates well and fails in
   deployment. Ask the user.
3. **wsitrain does not do registration.** If `registration_params.json` is
   missing, say so and point at `ST2WSI_Registration`.
4. **`--transform` must match between `segment` and `transfer`.** It selects
   which samples the run sees.
5. **`--gpus cpu` is segmentation-only.** There is no CPU-only CellViT training
   path.
6. **A stage command only exposes its own flags.** Everything else comes from
   `run-<tissue>.yaml`. Use `--show-config` before assuming a value.
7. **`--config` patches; `--reset-config` discards.** Use `--reset-config
   --config <saved.yaml>` to reproduce a run exactly.
8. **Stages skip when complete.** `--force` is required to redo one.
9. **The deliverable is `models/<tissue>/main/`**, used as
   `wsinsight --zoo-model-dir`. Report that path when a run finishes.

---

## 9. MCP Server (`wsinsight-train-mcp`)

Requires the `mcp` extra.

```bash
wsinsight-train-mcp                        # stdio (default)
wsinsight-train-mcp --http 127.0.0.1:8768  # streamable HTTP
```

| Option | Default | Description |
| ------ | ------- | ----------- |
| `--http HOST:PORT` | stdio | Serve over streamable HTTP. **There is no default port** — pass one explicitly. The family convention is 8768 (wsinsight 8765, sptxinsight 8766, hplot 8767). |

The tool surface is **reflected from the same argparse parser the CLI runs**, so
it cannot drift — the flag names in §4 are the argument names you pass, and
`--tissue` is present on every command.

**Job model.** Every command except `check` runs as a background job: the tool
returns a `job_id`. Poll `job_status` until it reports a terminal state, stream
with `job_logs`, stop with `cancel_job`. On failure, fetch `job_logs` before
retrying, and never re-issue the same job while a prior one is still running.
`check` runs synchronously — use it as the preflight before submitting a job.

`--no-stain-normalization` is exposed as an explicit `off_flag`: the setting
defaults to unset, so "off" cannot be expressed by omitting the parameter.
